# Copy to `.env.<alias>` — matching an alias in .firebaserc — and fill in. For local work,
# `.env.local`. Nothing here is secret.
#
# Which value goes where is not obvious: the Firebase console gives you a `firebaseConfig`
# block and four of its seven values are wanted, under different names. docs/deploying.html
# has the mapping, with where to find the block.
#
# The Firebase web config is public by design, and an OAuth client id for a browser app is
# not a credential either — what protects those accounts is the consent screen and the
# origin allowlist, not the value being hidden. `.env` and `.env.*` are still gitignored,
# because that is where a real secret ends up sooner or later.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- local development
#
# Deliberately NOT set here. `make up` passes VITE_USE_EMULATOR=1 as a shell variable, so it
# never needs to live in a file — and a file is where it does damage: `.env` is loaded for
# every mode, so one copied line would point every production build at localhost. If you do
# want it in a file, put it in `.env.local`, which is only ever yours.

# ------------------------------------------------------------------------ firebase
#
# Inlined into the bundle at build time, not read at runtime. So a build belongs to exactly
# one Firebase project: if you run this app for more than one group, each needs its own
# `.env.<group>` and its own `vite build --mode <group>` before its own deploy. Reusing one
# build across projects points a second group's site at the first group's data, and it looks
# entirely normal while it does so.
VITE_FIREBASE_API_KEY=
VITE_FIREBASE_AUTH_DOMAIN=
VITE_FIREBASE_PROJECT_ID=
VITE_FIREBASE_APP_ID=

# ------------------------------------------------------------------------ reminders
#
# Where a volunteer's personal link should point.
#
# Set this before sending anything real. Without it the app uses whatever address the page
# is being served from — which, running locally, means mailing out `localhost` links that
# nobody outside your machine can open. The reminder screen refuses to look happy about it,
# but it cannot know your production domain on its own.
VITE_PUBLIC_ORIGIN=https://your-app.web.app

# Sending from an organizer's own Gmail.
#
# Google Cloud console, on the same project as Firebase: enable the Gmail API, then create
# an OAuth 2.0 Client ID of type "Web application" with your hosting origin under
# "Authorized JavaScript origins". Leave the consent screen on External + Testing and add
# each organizer as a test user — `gmail.send` is a restricted scope, so publishing it
# would mean a security assessment, and Testing needs none. On a Google Workspace domain,
# publish the app Internal instead and consent stops expiring.
#
# To send while working locally, put this in `.env.local` and add `http://localhost:5173`
# to the same "Authorized JavaScript origins" list. Without that origin Google refuses the
# popup and the error names nothing useful. Reminders and invitations both send from here,
# and both will warn that the links they carry point at a machine nobody else can reach —
# fine for trying the sending itself out, not for anything real.
VITE_GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID=


# ------------------------------------------------------------------------ App Check
#
# Optional, and genuinely a judgement call rather than a box to tick.
#
# What it buys: the rules say what a request may do; App Check says whether the request came
# from this app at all. Without it, anybody who reads the Firebase config out of the built
# bundle — it is in there by design — can talk to the project with their own script, bound
# only by the rules.
#
# What it costs: on the web, App Check *is* reCAPTCHA. v3 or Enterprise, and there is no other
# provider. Setting this loads Google's reCAPTCHA on every page of the app, including a
# volunteer's pass, and lets it score whoever opened it — children included.
#
# Left blank, none of that loads and the rules are the whole defence, which here is a tight
# one: every write is gated behind the roster, and nothing is served publicly except one pass
# at a time by a token nobody can guess. Setting it adds a layer; leaving it blank is a
# defensible answer rather than a missing step.
#
# Order matters, or you lock yourself out of your own site:
#
#   1. Make the keys at google.com/recaptcha/admin — NOT in Firebase, which is the part that
#      catches people out. Add a site, type "Score based (v3)", and list your hosting domains
#      (your-project.web.app and your-project.firebaseapp.com). You get two keys back.
#   2. Paste the SECRET key into Firebase: console -> Build -> App Check -> Apps -> your web
#      app -> reCAPTCHA v3. That is the field that asks you for a key.
#   3. Put the SITE key — the other one — here, and deploy. Swap them by mistake and nothing
#      says so directly; the requests simply arrive unverified.
#   4. Leave it a day, then look at App Check -> APIs tab. It shows verified vs unverified
#      requests, so you can see your own traffic arriving with tokens before anything is
#      turned off.
#   5. Only then press Enforce, on Cloud Firestore first. Enforcing before step 1 has shipped
#      means every request from your own app is rejected.
#
# Ignored when running against the emulator, which does not enforce App Check.
VITE_APPCHECK_SITE_KEY=
